n<$Source01/rtl/a23_wishbone.v> u<1954> t<Top_level_rule> c<1> l<58:1> el<307:1>
  n<> u<1> t<Null_rule> p<1954> s<2> l<58:1> el<58:1>
  n<//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////> u<2> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<3> l<1:1> el<1:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<3> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<4> l<2:1> el<2:67>
  n<//  Wishbone master interface for the Amber core                //> u<4> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<5> l<3:1> el<3:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<5> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<6> l<4:1> el<4:67>
  n<//  This file is part of the Amber project                      //> u<6> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<7> l<5:1> el<5:67>
  n<//  http://www.opencores.org/project,amber                      //> u<7> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<8> l<6:1> el<6:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<8> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<9> l<7:1> el<7:67>
  n<//  Description                                                 //> u<9> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<10> l<8:1> el<8:67>
  n<//  Turns memory access requests from the execute stage and     //> u<10> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<11> l<9:1> el<9:67>
  n<//  cache into wishbone bus cycles. For 4-word read requests    //> u<11> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<12> l<10:1> el<10:67>
  n<//  from the cache and swap accesses ( read followed by write   //> u<12> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<13> l<11:1> el<11:67>
  n<//  to the same address) from the execute stage,                //> u<13> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<14> l<12:1> el<12:67>
  n<//  a block transfer is done. All other requests result in      //> u<14> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<15> l<13:1> el<13:67>
  n<//  single word transfers.                                      //> u<15> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<16> l<14:1> el<14:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<16> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<17> l<15:1> el<15:67>
  n<//  Write accesses can be done in a single clock cycle on       //> u<17> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<18> l<16:1> el<16:67>
  n<//  the wishbone bus, is the destination allows it. The         //> u<18> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<19> l<17:1> el<17:67>
  n<//  next transfer will begin immediately on the                 //> u<19> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<20> l<18:1> el<18:67>
  n<//  next cycle on the bus. This looks like a block transfer     //> u<20> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<21> l<19:1> el<19:67>
  n<//  and does hold ownership of the wishbone bus, preventing     //> u<21> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<22> l<20:1> el<20:67>
  n<//  the other master ( the ethernet MAC) from gaining           //> u<22> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<23> l<21:1> el<21:67>
  n<//  ownership between those two cycles. But otherwise it would  //> u<23> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<24> l<22:1> el<22:67>
  n<//  be necessary to insert a wait cycle after every write,      //> u<24> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<25> l<23:1> el<23:67>
  n<//  slowing down the performance of the core by around 5 to     //> u<25> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<26> l<24:1> el<24:67>
  n<//  10%.                                                        //> u<26> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<27> l<25:1> el<25:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<27> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<28> l<26:1> el<26:67>
  n<//  Author(s):                                                  //> u<28> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<29> l<27:1> el<27:67>
  n<//      - Conor Santifort, csantifort.amber@gmail.com           //> u<29> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<30> l<28:1> el<28:67>
  n<//                                                              //> u<30> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<1954> s<31> l<29:1> el<29:67>
